  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity and resilience of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In May 2023, the Labour inspection Unit of the Ministry of Labour improved efficiency of data collection, storage and reporting processes through digitalization of forms and documents and assignment of handheld data processing devices to labour inspectors and other officers.  This also complemented its action to fully operationalize the Labour Market Information System (LMIS) and Labour Administration Application (LAA 2.0), for more seamless sharing of information, easier generation of reports and statistical analysis of labour market data from various sources. Between August- September 2022, the ILO with support from the Government of Brazil, provided technical assistance to the Ministry and Central Statistical Office through knowledge-sharing interventions on methodologies for child labour inspections, collection and analysis of data on child labour. This included virtual training and study visit to Brazil to observe the new census rollout and bilateral knowledge exchange on developing modular approach to CL data collection through periodic household and labour force surveys. In May 2023 the ILO facilitated procurement of 16 handheld tablets and key accessories for the labour inspectorate unit to support the Ministry&#x2019;s digitalization process.

3.In June 2023 the ILO gave technical advisory to the working groups of the National Steering Committee on Child Labour to support processes for development of hazardous child labour lists, formulation of CL policy and standard operating procedures for collaboration with partner institutions and for development of a country acceleration (action) plan on child labour.</narrative>
   </description>
